10:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L18",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about 2:30 p.m. to 6 p.m.; later interpretation of the battle","place":"The Hornet’s Nest, the ridge west of Pittsburg Landing, Dill Branch, and the Tennessee River, Tennessee","result":"Grant ordered chief of staff Joseph D. Webster to build a final defensive line near Pittsburg Landing. Webster placed five 24-pounder siege guns near the Corinth Road and collected field artillery around them. Hurlbut, Sherman, McClernand, and survivors from Wallace’s and Prentiss’s divisions joined the line as they withdrew. By late afternoon, the position extended about 2,500 yards from the river and held roughly 18,000 men according to the episode; the NPS estimates nearly 20,000 before Buell’s troops landed. The gunboats Tyler and Lexington supported the left flank. The Hornet’s Nest stand delayed some Confederate formations, but the final line was already strong before its defenders surrendered, and modern historians dispute the older claim that the stand alone saved Grant’s army","facts":[{"label":"Final line started","value":"About 2:30 p.m. under Colonel Joseph D. Webster"},{"label":"Siege artillery","value":"Five 24-pounder guns placed near the Corinth Road"},{"label":"Field artillery","value":"Podcast estimate: about 40 additional guns; NPS total: about 50 guns"},{"label":"Line length","value":"About 2,500 yards"},{"label":"Infantry strength","value":"Podcast estimate: about 18,000; NPS: nearly 20,000 before Buell landed"},{"label":"Left flank","value":"Anchored on the Tennessee River above Pittsburg Landing"},{"label":"Right flank","value":"Sherman facing southwest and covering Lew Wallace’s expected approach"},{"label":"Naval support","value":"Timberclads Tyler and Lexington, using 8-inch and 32-pounder guns"}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map shows Pittsburg Landing, the Corinth Road, Dill Branch, the Hornet’s Nest, and the roads used by the withdrawing divisions.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"Why the Hornet’s Nest was cut off","caption":"Sherman and McClernand withdrew from the west and Hurlbut from the east. Wallace and Prentiss remained until Confederate forces closed their escape routes.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/upload/Hornet-s-Nest-2026.pdf","image":"/images/shiloh-hornets-nest-collapse.svg","alt":"Map of the collapse and encirclement of the Hornet’s Nest"},{"title":"Grant’s final defensive line","caption":"Webster anchored the center on massed artillery near the Corinth Road. The line curved from the river west and north, with Hurlbut near the guns, McClernand and Sherman to the right, and gunboats covering Dill Branch.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/shiloh-auto-tour-introduction-grant-s-last-line-stop-1.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-grants-last-line.svg","alt":"Map of Grant’s final line and naval support near Pittsburg Landing"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"The 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the battlefield roads, fields, camps, and the ground between the Hornet’s Nest and Pittsburg Landing.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:09","audioTime":69,"title":"The episode reviews the Hornet’s Nest surrender","detail":"Wallace was mortally wounded, and Prentiss and about 2,200 men from the 2nd and 6th Divisions were captured."},{"time":"2:16","audioTime":136,"title":"Prentiss promoted a postwar interpretation","detail":"Prentiss argued that the Hornet’s Nest stand gave Grant time to build the final defensive line and therefore saved the army."},{"time":"3:27","audioTime":207,"title":"Both flanks had withdrawn by about 4:30 p.m.","detail":"Sherman, McClernand, and Hurlbut moved toward the final line while Wallace and Prentiss continued holding the center."},{"time":"4:54","audioTime":294,"title":"The final line was already strong by 3 p.m.","detail":"Additional withdrawn units continued joining the position before the Hornet’s Nest surrendered."},{"time":"5:16","audioTime":316,"title":"The stand delayed the Confederate concentration","detail":"Confederate forces focused on surrounding the center, but the captured defenders were then unavailable to reinforce Grant’s line."},{"time":"6:32","audioTime":392,"title":"David W. Reed shaped later battlefield interpretation","detail":"The wounded 12th Iowa veteran later became historian of the Shiloh park commission and emphasized the Hornet’s Nest in his published history."},{"time":"8:52","audioTime":532,"title":"The episode examines Grant’s responsibility","detail":"Grant’s last order to Prentiss was to hold. The episode argues that he should have sent a new order after the adjacent divisions withdrew."},{"time":"10:15","audioTime":615,"title":"Webster was ordered to form the last line","detail":"Grant assigned his chief of staff to collect artillery and available infantry near Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"13:35","audioTime":815,"title":"Webster positioned the siege guns","detail":"Starting around 2:30 p.m., he placed five 24-pounders across the likely approach along the Corinth Road and added available field batteries."},{"time":"15:26","audioTime":926,"title":"Withdrawn divisions joined the line","detail":"Hurlbut rallied his division near the artillery. Sherman and McClernand formed farther right, and escaped fragments of the 2nd and 6th Divisions also joined."},{"time":"16:17","audioTime":977,"title":"The final position extended from the river","detail":"The line ran about 2,500 yards west from the Tennessee River and curved north, with Sherman protecting the outer right."},{"time":"17:14","audioTime":1034,"title":"The gunboats supported the left flank","detail":"Tyler and Lexington engaged Confederate batteries, then positioned near Dill Branch where their guns could fire into the ravine."},{"time":"19:20","audioTime":1160,"title":"Confederates reorganized after the surrender","detail":"Bragg and Polk tried to separate units tangled during the Hornet’s Nest encirclement and move prisoners to the rear."},{"time":"20:09","audioTime":1209,"title":"Chalmers and Jackson attacked near 6 p.m.","detail":"Their brigades advanced toward the United States left with less than twenty minutes remaining before sunset and without a fully coordinated supporting attack."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Ordered the final line formed and left naval fire decisions to the gunboat commanders"},{"commander":"Joseph D. Webster","command":"Chief of staff and artillery coordinator","subordinates":"Placed the siege guns, collected field batteries, and organized the defensive position near Pittsburg Landing"},{"commander":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Rallied infantry to support Webster’s artillery"},{"commander":"John A. McClernand","command":"1st Division","subordinates":"Held the central-right section after withdrawing"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"5th Division","subordinates":"Held the right flank and covered the route expected from Lew Wallace"},{"commander":"William Gwin / James W. Shirk","command":"USS Tyler / USS Lexington","subordinates":"Silenced Confederate batteries and fired toward Dill Branch"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Commanded the army after Albert Sidney Johnston’s death"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg / Leonidas Polk","command":"Late-afternoon field direction","subordinates":"Reorganized units after the Hornet’s Nest surrender and prepared another advance"},{"commander":"James R. Chalmers / John K. Jackson","command":"Brigades on the Confederate right","subordinates":"Advanced toward Dill Branch and the United States left near 6 p.m."}]}],"people":[{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Ordered a final defensive line near Pittsburg Landing but did not send Wallace and Prentiss a documented withdrawal order"},{"name":"Joseph D. Webster","role":"Grant’s chief of staff; organized the massed artillery and infantry line above Pittsburg Landing","side":"United States"},{"name":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","role":"Surrendered at the Hornet’s Nest and later promoted the claim that the defense saved Grant’s army","side":"United States","image":"/images/benjamin-prentiss.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666477/"},{"name":"W.H.L. Wallace","role":"Held with Prentiss and was mortally wounded after ordering a late withdrawal","side":"United States"},{"name":"David W. Reed","role":"12th Iowa veteran and later Shiloh park commission historian whose work emphasized the Hornet’s Nest","side":"United States"},{"name":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","role":"Rallied withdrawn troops as infantry support for Webster’s artillery","side":"United States"},{"name":"William Gwin","role":"Commanded USS Tyler and used its heavy guns against Confederate batteries and troops near Dill Branch","side":"United States"},{"name":"James W. Shirk","role":"Commanded USS Lexington and joined Tyler off the United States left","side":"United States"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Tried to reorganize Confederate troops and prepare a final assault before dark","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/braxton-bragg.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670042/"},{"name":"Leonidas Polk","role":"Helped push Confederate units toward Grant’s final position","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/leonidas-polk.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670125/"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brigadier General W.H.L. Wallace","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 2nd Division, Army of the Tennessee","outcome":"Mortally wounded during the Hornet’s Nest withdrawal on April 6; died April 10"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing in 1862","caption":"Grant’s final line occupied the ridge immediately west and southwest of the landing.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"Ravines protected the approaches to the final line and limited coordinated Confederate movement.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/benjamin-prentiss.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Benjamin M. Prentiss","caption":"Prentiss’s postwar accounts helped establish the traditional interpretation of the Hornet’s Nest.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666477/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 122","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/122-shiloh-part-the-twelfth"},{"label":"National Park Service: Grant’s Last Line","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/shiloh-auto-tour-introduction-grant-s-last-line-stop-1.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Grant’s Left Flank","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/grant-s-left-flank-tour-stop-21.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Hornet’s Nest, myth and evidence","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/upload/Hornet-s-Nest-2026.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"}]}}]}]
